In <9305231515.AA04768@albert.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu wrote:
>
>   Let me just state in Mike's defense that most of the wormhole stuff
> is peer-reviewed physics. Tipler, Wheeler, Thorne, Visser, and others
> have worked out legitimate solutions in general relativity that
> permit closed-timelike-loops. (and FTL)

These are legitimate mathematically, but as Tipler, etc say, they involve
physically unreasonable matter.   Known physics is that there is no known way
to traverse a wormhole.  (Except as a one way trip to the end of time or to
the big crunch.)  No impossibility proof has been made against portals, but
no counter examples have been found either.

Solutions involving closed timelike curves, reasonable matter, and a
reasonable external universe do not go anywhere - what they say is that
inside the heart of a supernova, in the process of forming a black hole,
things might get thrown a few milliseconds back in time.  Not the same
thing as a portal or a time tunnel.

