Date: Sun, 23 May 93 23:27:34 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Traversable Wormholes

James A. Donald says:

>>   Let me just state in Mike's defense that most of the wormhole stuff
>> is peer-reviewed physics. Tipler, Wheeler, Thorne, Visser, and others
>> have worked out legitimate solutions in general relativity that
>> permit closed-timelike-loops. (and FTL)
> 
> These are legitimate mathematically, but as Tipler, etc say, they
> involve physically unreasonable matter.

How do you decide what is "physically unreasonable"?  I'm not sure who
"etc" is.  Certainly not Thorne and Visser.  They are quite explicit
that exotic matter is not illegitimate.  Tipler takes the contrary view,
but his reasoning is often self-deluding.  Remember the wise words of
Steven Weinberg (Nobel Laureate):

     That is often the way it is in physics - our mistake is not
     that we take our theories too seriously, but that we do not
     take them seriously enough.

Physics Prof Gerald Feinberg once said, whilst talking about the
feasibility of cryonics, that the only way to assess limits to
technology is to examine physical conservation laws.  Unless some
postulated technological feat violates a law of physics then it is
wisest to assume it is achievable.  Of all the concepts I've come across
this is one of the most profound and also the hardest for ordinary
people to accept.  It has a similar status to the notion of wealth
creation in economics.  (Most people believe that wealth isn't created
but merely passed from one to another or 'stolen from the Earth'. 
Council of Rome crap and all that.)

This is the lesson of history.  Leonardo saw the birds fly and knew that
humans would one day fly, although none of his design plans would have
worked.  He had the right idea, though.  He used induction.  What nature
can do, so will we.  Don't allow your own imagination to limit what you
believe to be possible.  Take your cue from physics and nature.  There
is no reason to believe that our minds should be capable of grasping the
level of complexity our technology will achieve.  No more reason than
to suppose that a beetle can comprehend the universe it lives in, as JBS
Haldane put it.

The universe is not limited by us.  We are limited only by the universe. 
Most get this the worng way round.

>  No impossibility proof has been made against portals, but no counter
> examples have been found either.

Yet.  When an impossibility proof is found then I'll ditch the idea of
traversable wormholes.  Not until.
> 
> James A. Donald

