From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: CHALLENGE: Chaitin and RTFM

Apparently the CHALLENGE is still afoot, with Mike assuming that he "won"
while I, at least, had perceived the opposite.

My understanding was that Mike had claimed that Chaitin had "clouded the
distinction between undecidable and computationally difficult" by claiming that
Omega was uncomputable when actually it was just hard to compute.

I base this on his statement,

"More helpfully, so that people can see where I'm coming from, I'll
demonstrate that Chaitin _is_ saying that it is [hard]...  and _not_
that it is impossible.  The easiest way...  is to see that the solving
the expansion of Omega is not impossible, but analogous to the
calculation of expansion of pi...."

However, he and Derek later agreed that Omega was in fact uncomputable,
not just hard to compute, and that there was a fundamental difference
between calculating the expansion of Omega and calculating the expansion of
pi.

Apparently this is not how Mike sees it.  He argued that he "won" the
challenge, based on a quote from Chaitin's article.

He takes the example Derek quoted to demonstrate this, about which Derek
wrote, "I admit that the wording is unfortunate...."  However, Mike did
not produce an argument for why this quotation clouded the distinction
between undecidable and computationally difficult.  Instead, he proceeded
to make the argument above about Omega being like pi, an argument which
was incorrect.

Is Mike perhaps now arguing that Chaitin "clouded" this issue by writing
"cloudily", that is, unclearly, and therefore Derek's comment about
"unfortunate" wording is sufficient to make his case on its own?  I
have the impression that this is a weak form of Mike's original statement.
Everyone writes unclearly from time to time, but to accuse them therefore
of clouding an important distinction requires in my mind a more deliberate
attempt at obfuscation or a more fundamental misunderstanding.

Unless Mike can demonstrate more clearly exactly what was clouded in
Chaitin's statements and what the true facts are about what he was writing
then I would judge that he has not adequately answered the CHALLENGE.

I was off the list when the AS was created.  What are the rules for resolving
disputes like this?

Hal

